@ifnottex
@node Top
@top readq: incremental reading for Emacs

readq keeps a queue of everything you are reading through (books@comma{}
lecture recordings@comma{} web pages) and tells you what to read next.  It
remembers where you stopped in each one.  Passages worth keeping become
@strong{extracts}@comma{} short notes that come back for review until you turn them
into flashcards.

The routine is always the same:

@enumerate
@item
@code{C-c r n} opens what you should read now.
@item
Read for a while.  Select a passage worth keeping and press @code{C-c r e}.
@item
@code{C-c r n} again.  readq saves your place and opens the next item.
@end enumerate

This manual shows how to do each part.  It uses @code{C-c r} as readq's
prefix key.  In Emacs@comma{} open it with @code{C-h R readq} or @code{C-c r h}; in it@comma{}
@code{i} looks up a command or option by name and @code{s} searches the text.

@end ifnottex

