@node Installing
@section Installing

@findex readq-mode
@vindex readq-mode

readq is a folder of Emacs Lisp files.  It needs Emacs 27.1 or later@comma{}
and two small packages of the same author: @uref{https://github.com/n0w1ht/card-export, card-export} and @uref{https://github.com/n0w1ht/readq-comic, readq-comic}.
Put all three folders on your load path:

@lisp
(use-package readq
  :load-path ("~/src/card-export" "~/src/readq-comic" "~/src/readq")
  :config (readq-mode 1)
  :bind-keymap ("C-c r" . readq-command-map))
@end lisp

@samp{readq-mode} is what follows you as you read: it brings each book
back to your place and counts your reading time.

Everything else is optional.  Install only what you read with:

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem To read
@tab Install
@item PDFs
@tab pdf-tools (or pdf-canvas@comma{} or use @samp{doc-view})
@item EPUBs
@tab nov.el
@item Audio@comma{} video@comma{} YouTube
@tab mpv@comma{} and yt-dlp for online videos
@item Comics
@tab 7-Zip@comma{} unar@comma{} or unzip for CBZ only
@item PDFs outside Emacs
@tab SumatraPDF (Windows)
@item Fast extract search
@tab consult and ripgrep
@item Flashcards
@tab org-srs@comma{} or Anki with the AnkiConnect add-on
@item Reading aloud
@tab kokoro.el
@end multitable

@node Checking your setup
@section Checking your setup

@findex readq-doctor

Run @code{C-c r ?} (@samp{readq-doctor}) once after installing@comma{} and again
whenever something stops working.  It checks every program readq uses
and says how to fix what is missing:

@example
ok  Database    ~/notes/readq.eld: 42 books@comma{} 310 extracts
ok  pdf-tools   epdfinfo works
XX  nov.el      cannot find unzip (`nov-unzip-program')
--  Anki        AnkiConnect does not answer; start Anki before exporting
@end example

@samp{XX} is a problem with something you use@comma{} @samp{!!} a warning@comma{} @samp{--} a note
about something you don't use yet.  Press @code{g} to check again.

@node Your first ten minutes
@section Your first ten minutes

@findex readq-add-book
@findex readq-add-directory
@findex readq-next
@findex readq-extract
@findex readq

@enumerate
@item
@code{C-c r a} adds a file.  Give it a priority from @strong{0 (most important)
to 100 (least)}@comma{} and tags if you like@comma{} such as @samp{cardio}.  @code{C-c r A}
adds every book in a folder.
@item
@code{C-c r n} opens the book to read now.  Read a few pages.
@item
Select a sentence worth keeping and press @code{C-c r e}.  It is now an
extract.
@item
@code{C-c r n} again.  Your place is saved and the next item opens.
@item
@code{C-c r l} shows the whole queue.
@end enumerate

You can also open a queued file the usual way@comma{} with @code{C-x C-f} or Dired:
readq recognizes it and puts you back where you were.

