@node The daily loop
@section The daily loop

@findex readq-next
@findex readq-finish-session
@findex readq-open
@findex readq-suggest
@findex readq

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Key
@tab What it does
@item @code{C-c r n}
@tab finish what you are reading and open the next due item
@item @code{C-u C-c r n}
@tab the same@comma{} but only from some tags (e.g. only @samp{#cardio})
@item @code{C-c r f}
@tab finish the session without opening anything
@item @code{C-c r o}
@tab open an item you choose
@item @code{C-c r s}
@tab list what is due today@comma{} without opening anything
@item @code{C-c r l}
@tab the dashboard: the whole queue
@end multitable

A session counts@comma{} and the item is scheduled again@comma{} if you moved forward
or read for at least a minute.  Just glancing at a book changes nothing.

When you reach the end of a book@comma{} readq offers to mark it finished.

@node How often things come back
@section How often things come back

@findex readq-set-priority
@findex readq-priority-up
@findex readq-priority-down

Priority decides it.  @strong{Lower numbers come first and come back sooner.}

@itemize
@item
@strong{Books@comma{} chapters and recordings} come back at a steady rhythm: every
day at priority 0@comma{} about once a week at priority 100.
@item
@strong{Extracts} come back after growing gaps: a few days@comma{} then weeks@comma{} then
months.  The less important@comma{} the faster the gap grows.
@end itemize

Change a priority with @code{C-c r =}@comma{} or nudge it with @code{C-c r +} and
@code{C-c r -}.  It is the main thing you tune.

@node When it gets too much
@section When it gets too much

@findex readq-next
@findex readq-postpone
@findex readq-reschedule
@findex readq-toggle-pause
@findex readq-toggle-finished
@findex readq-workload

readq plans each day up to a @strong{budget}@comma{} 90 minutes by default.  What
doesn't fit moves to the next days@comma{} least important first.  Items of
priority 10 or lower always stay.  Once the budget is used up@comma{} @code{C-c r n}
asks before opening more.

@lisp
(setq readq-daily-minutes 60)   ; or nil for no limit
@end lisp

For one item at a time:

@multitable {aaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Key
@tab What it does
@item @code{C-c r z}
@tab postpone it (@code{C-u}: by a number of days)
@item @code{C-c r r}
@tab read it again in N days
@item @code{C-c r x}
@tab pause it until you press @code{x} again
@item @code{C-c r F}
@tab mark it finished (or not)
@item @code{C-c r L}
@tab see today's budget and the load of the next days
@end multitable

@node Deadlines and exams
@section Deadlines and exams

@findex readq-set-deadline
@findex readq-review-subset
@findex readq-focus
@findex readq-set-tags
@findex readq-exam
@findex readq-workload

@itemize
@item
@code{C-c r !} sets a date by which to finish a book or chapter.  readq
works out the pace per day and keeps it coming back often enough.
A deadline beats the daily budget.
@item
@code{C-u C-c r !} sets a deadline for a whole tag@comma{} such as @samp{#cardio}.
@item
@code{C-c r b} reviews one book@comma{} chapter@comma{} extract or tag @strong{now}@comma{} due or
not: the cram before an exam.  @code{C-c r n} goes through it in order;
@code{C-u C-c r b} stops.
@item
@code{C-c r /} sets a @strong{focus}: until you clear it@comma{} only items with those
tags are suggested.  @code{C-c r #} sets an item's tags.
@end itemize

@strong{Exam mode.}  @code{C-c r t} asks for an exam's name@comma{} its date and the tags it
covers@comma{} and plans back from the date:

@enumerate
@item
@emph{Reading}@comma{} until ten days before: everything with those tags gets
that day as its deadline@comma{} so readq sets the pace.
@item
@emph{Extracts only}@comma{} for the next seven days: the exam's books stay
closed and each of its extracts comes up at least once.  Turn them
into cards.
@item
@emph{Cards only}@comma{} for the last three days: nothing of the exam comes up
in readq.  Review your cards.
@end enumerate

Over the last two weeks the daily budget grows@comma{} to one and a half
times on the day.  @code{C-c r L} shows where each exam stands.  To drop an
exam@comma{} press @code{C-c r t}@comma{} choose it and give no date.  The lengths are
@code{readq-exam-extract-days}@comma{} @code{readq-exam-card-days}@comma{} @code{readq-exam-ramp-days}
and @code{readq-exam-budget-factor}.

@node The dashboard
@section The dashboard

@findex readq
@findex readq-stats

@code{C-c r l} shows the queue as a tree: chapters and extracts under their
book@comma{} sub-extracts under their extract.  Rows are in the order you
should read them.  The mode line sums it up@comma{} for example
@samp{3 due (1 extracts)@comma{} 40 in queue — today 52/90 min}.

The dashboard uses the same letters as @code{C-c r} (=@comma{} z@comma{} x@comma{} #@comma{} b@comma{} L@dots{})@comma{}
on the row at point.  A few are its own:

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Key
@tab What it does
@item @code{RET}
@tab open the item@comma{} or fold a chapter
@item @code{TAB} / @code{S-TAB}
@tab fold this row / all rows
@item @code{N}
@tab read next
@item @code{t}
@tab rename the item
@item @code{l}
@tab point it to its file again@comma{} after you moved it
@item @code{D}
@tab remove a book from the queue
@item @code{.}
@tab show or hide extracts
@item @code{H}
@tab show or hide finished books
@item @code{G}
@tab redraw
@end multitable

@code{C-c r %} shows reading stats: minutes per day@comma{} streak@comma{} pages@comma{} and
time by book and tag (@code{w}@comma{} @code{m}@comma{} @code{y} for week@comma{} month@comma{} year).

