@node One chapter at a time
@section One chapter at a time

@findex readq-add-sections

A big textbook works better as chapters@comma{} each its own item with its
own priority.  Press @code{C-c r T} on the book: readq lists its table of
contents.  Mark the chapters you want with @code{m} (or @code{=} to give one its
own priority)@comma{} then press @code{x} to add them.  readq offers to pause the
book itself@comma{} so only the chosen chapters come up.

A chapter keeps its own place; reading it doesn't move the book's
bookmark.  For a PDF without bookmarks@comma{} @samp{M-x readq-add-section} adds a
chapter by its page numbers.

@node PDFs in SumatraPDF
@section PDFs in SumatraPDF

@vindex readq-default-pdf-viewer
@findex readq-set-viewer

On Windows you can read a PDF or comic in SumatraPDF instead of Emacs:
@code{C-c r v} switches the item at point@comma{} and @samp{readq-default-pdf-viewer}
sets it for all.  readq opens SumatraPDF at your page@comma{} and learns the
new page when you @strong{close the document}.  Highlights you save into the
PDF there become extracts (see @ref{Highlights from other PDF viewers}).

