@node Making an extract
@section Making an extract

@findex readq-extract

Select the passage and press @code{C-c r e}.  That works in pdf-tools@comma{}
pdf-canvas@comma{} nov.el@comma{} eww@comma{} Org@comma{} Markdown and text@comma{} and inside an extract
(which makes a @strong{sub-extract}).  In a PDF@comma{} the passage is also
highlighted and saved into the file.  @code{C-u C-c r e} asks for a priority.

Several passages at once: in a PDF@comma{} drag with @code{C-} held for each; with
the multi-region package@comma{} any selection it holds.  They become one
extract (@code{C-u C-u C-c r e}: one each).

@node Highlight keep or review
@section Highlight@comma{} keep or review

@findex readq-highlight
@findex readq-extract-keep
@findex readq-extract
@findex readq-find-highlight
@findex readq-toggle-finished
Not every passage deserves a place in the queue.  Select it@comma{} then:

@multitable {aaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@item @code{C-c r H}
@tab @strong{highlight} it and nothing more
@item @code{C-c r E}
@tab make an extract that is @strong{kept} as a note but never comes up
@item @code{C-c r e}
@tab make an extract that comes back for @strong{review}
@end multitable

A highlight in a PDF is saved into the PDF@comma{} in green; in an EPUB@comma{} a
text or a web page@comma{} readq remembers it and shows it when you read
there.  @code{C-c r J} lists the highlights of a book and jumps to one;
@code{C-u C-c r H} on a highlight removes it.

You can change your mind later.  On a highlight@comma{} with nothing selected@comma{}
@code{C-c r e} or @code{C-c r E} makes it an extract.  On a kept extract@comma{} @code{C-c r F}
puts it in the queue.

@node Reviewing extracts
@section Reviewing extracts

@findex readq-goto-source
@findex readq-open
@findex readq-edit-extract
@findex readq-dismiss
@findex readq-mark-ready

When @code{C-c r n} brings up an extract@comma{} it opens its note.  Then:

@itemize
@item
@strong{Shorten it.}  Delete what you don't need and rewrite the rest.  Each
review boils it down a little.  This is the heart of incremental
reading.
@item
@code{C-c r e} on part of it makes a sub-extract.
@item
@code{C-c r g} shows the passage in the book.  Your bookmark stays where it
was; @code{C-c r o} goes back to it.
@item
@code{C-c r '} edits the extract in a window beside the book.
@item
@code{C-c r d} dismisses it: out of the queue@comma{} note kept.
@item
@code{C-c r c} marks it ready for a flashcard (see @ref{Flashcards}).
@item
@code{C-c r n} moves on.
@end itemize

@node Editing an extract beside the book
@section Editing an extract beside the book

@vindex readq-click-opens-extract
@vindex readq-edit-extract-note-commands
@vindex readq-edit-extract-side
@vindex readq-edit-extract-size
@findex readq-edit-extract

While reading@comma{} @code{C-c r '} opens an extract's note in a window beside the
book.  Which one:

@itemize
@item
in an EPUB@comma{} a text or a web page@comma{} the extract highlighted where the
cursor is;
@item
in a PDF@comma{} the one on the page shown@comma{} or one you choose when there
are several.  Quicker: @strong{click its highlight} and its note opens.
Clicking other highlights does what pdf-tools does.  To turn this
off@comma{} set @samp{readq-click-opens-extract} to nil.
@end itemize

Edits go straight into the note.  @code{C-c C-c} saves it and closes the
window; if you changed the title@comma{} the file is renamed.  Editing doesn't
count as a review or change when the extract comes back.

Denote's commands work in that window: @code{C-c n k} to change keywords@comma{}
@code{C-c n l} to insert a link@comma{} and so on.  They act on the extract's
note.  This holds for commands called with a key or from a menu@comma{} not
with @code{M-x}.  Keywords you add are kept; readq's own (@samp{extract} and the
book's tags) come back if you remove them.  Other commands can be
added through @samp{readq-edit-extract-note-commands}.

The window is on the right@comma{} 40% of the frame wide: see
@samp{readq-edit-extract-side} and @samp{readq-edit-extract-size}.

@node Where extracts are stored
@section Where extracts are stored

@vindex readq-extracts-directory
@findex readq-visit-extracts

Each extract is one Org file in @samp{~/notes/}@comma{} named the way @uref{https://protesilaos.com/emacs/denote, Denote} names
notes@comma{} so Denote@comma{} consult-notes or plain Dired all work with them:

@example
20261115T142233==guyton=physiology=p0057--systole-is-the-phase__cardio_extract.org
@end example

@example
#+title:      Systole is the phase of contraction.
#+filetags:   :cardio:extract:
#+identifier: 20261115T142233
#+signature:  guyton=physiology=p0057
#+source:     [[readq:20261115T142233][Guyton Physiology@comma{} p. 57]]

#+begin_quote
Systole is the phase of contraction.
#+end_quote
@end example

The title can be anything; edit it and the file is renamed.  @code{C-c r Z}
lists a book's notes in Dired@comma{} in book order.  To keep them elsewhere@comma{}
set @samp{readq-extracts-directory}.

@menu
* Silos::
@end menu

@node Silos
@subsection Silos

@vindex readq-silos
@findex readq-set-silo

A book can keep its extracts in a @uref{https://protesilaos.com/emacs/denote, Denote silo} of its own@comma{} a separate
notes folder@comma{} so that pathology extracts go to @samp{~/Pathology/notes/} and
the rest to @samp{~/notes/}.  The silos are those of
@samp{denote-silo-directories}@comma{} or of @samp{readq-silos} if you set it.

A book you add gets the silo whose parent folder holds the book's file:
with @samp{~/Pathology/notes/} a silo@comma{} a book in @samp{~/Pathology/Breast/} keeps
its extracts there.  @code{C-c r @@} (@samp{readq-set-silo})@comma{} or @code{@@} on the
dashboard@comma{} chooses another one; the notes already made move with it.
All books stay in the one queue.

Coming from readq 0.1?  @samp{M-x readq-convert-extracts} turns your old
extracts into notes like these.  The old files are left alone.

@node Figures
@section Figures

@findex readq-extract-figure
@findex readq-extract-figure-from-clipboard

@code{C-c r P} makes a diagram or picture an extract.  In a PDF@comma{} select the
area with @code{M-drag} first; in an EPUB@comma{} web page or Org file@comma{} put point on
the image; in a comic@comma{} it takes the page.  Pictures copied elsewhere
(SumatraPDF@comma{} a browser) are taken from the clipboard with @code{C-c r y}.

Figures are saved in @samp{~/notes/figures/} and shown in the extract.  They
go to Anki and org-srs with the card.

