@node Cleaning up
@section Cleaning up

@findex readq-stale-extracts
@findex readq-delete-extract
@findex readq-dismiss

Extracts you keep postponing pile up.  @code{C-c r X} walks through the
@strong{stale} ones@comma{} reviewed often or long ago without becoming anything@comma{} and
asks for each: make it a card@comma{} merge it into another@comma{} dismiss@comma{} delete@comma{}
or keep.

@code{C-c r D} deletes an extract with its note and its highlight in the
PDF@.  @code{C-c r d} only takes it out of the queue.

@node Highlights from other PDF viewers
@section Highlights from other PDF viewers

@findex readq-import-highlights
@findex readq-import-all-highlights

Highlights saved into a PDF by SumatraPDF@comma{} Okular or a tablet app
become extracts: @code{C-c r i} for the book at point@comma{} @code{C-c r I} for all PDFs.
readq also looks for new ones before suggesting what to read.  Needs
pdf-tools.

@node Flashcards
@chapter Flashcards

@findex readq-cloze
@findex readq-mark-ready
@findex readq-export-cards
@findex readq-update-anki-cards

Once an extract holds just one fact@comma{} make it a card:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Shape it.}  For a cloze@comma{} select the words to hide and press
@code{C-c r k} (@code{C-u} adds a hint).  Otherwise the extract's @strong{title is the
question} and its text the answer: rewrite the title as a question.
@item
@strong{Mark it ready} with @code{C-c r c}.  It leaves the reading queue.
@item
@strong{Export} all ready extracts with @code{C-c r C}.
@end enumerate

readq asks where to send them@comma{} or set it once:

@lisp
(setq readq-flashcard-backend 'anki)   ; or 'org-srs
@end lisp

@itemize
@item
@strong{Anki:} keep Anki open with the AnkiConnect add-on (code
@samp{2055492159}).  Cards go into the deck @samp{readq::<book>}.  If you edit an
extract later@comma{} @code{C-c r U} updates its card and keeps its reviews.
Without AnkiConnect@comma{} readq writes a file for @emph{File > Import}.
@item
@strong{org-srs:} cards go into @samp{<book>-cards.org} next to your notes.  Review
them with @samp{M-x org-srs-review-start}.
@end itemize

@menu
* Drafts from a local model::
* Cards you keep failing::
@end menu

@node Drafts from a local model
@section Drafts from a local model

@findex readq-draft-cards
@findex readq-mark-ready
If you use gptel@comma{} @code{C-c r Q} on an extract asks its model for three card
drafts@comma{} clozes and questions@comma{} from the extract's text.  Choose one: it
is written into the note for you to correct@comma{} then @code{C-c r c} as usual.
The text goes only to the model gptel is set up with@comma{} so with a local
model nothing leaves your computer.  Drafts can be wrong: check them
against the passage.  @code{readq-draft-count} and @code{readq-draft-instructions}
change what is asked.

@node Cards you keep failing
@section Cards you keep failing

A card failed three times was learned without its context.  Its
extract then comes back into the reading queue@comma{} due today@comma{} and opens
with the passage in the book beside it: reread@comma{} rework the extract@comma{}
@code{C-c r n}.  With org-srs@comma{} readq counts each ``again''.  With Anki@comma{} it asks
once a day while Anki is running (@samp{M-x readq-check-failed-cards} asks
now).  @code{readq-card-lapse-threshold} sets the number; @samp{nil} turns it off.

@node Your data
@chapter Your data

@findex readq-restore-backup

Everything readq keeps is in @samp{~/notes/}:

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem File
@tab Holds
@item @samp{readq.eld}
@tab the queue: priorities@comma{} dates@comma{} places@comma{} history
@item @samp{readq-backups/}
@tab a copy of the queue from each of the last 14 days
@item @samp{*__extract.org}
@tab your extracts@comma{} one note each
@item @samp{figures/}
@tab the pictures of figure extracts
@end multitable

Back up the whole folder.  If the queue file is ever damaged@comma{} @code{C-c r B}
puts back a daily copy.  The queue file is plain text@comma{} so it syncs well@comma{}
but don't run readq on two computers at the same time.

If you do@comma{} readq notices.  When another program@comma{} such as a second Emacs
or a sync program@comma{} changed the queue file since readq read it@comma{} readq
does not save over it: it says so once@comma{} and keeps what you do in this
Emacs without saving it.  Then choose which queue to keep:

@itemize
@item
@samp{M-x readq-reload-database} reads the file as it is now.  What you
did in this Emacs since is dropped.
@item
@samp{M-x readq-save-database} saves this Emacs's queue over the file@comma{}
after asking.  The other program's changes are dropped.
@end itemize

If you close Emacs before choosing@comma{} it asks you.

